\chapter{Module A: Genetic Dysregulation}

\section{Overview}

Module A models the dynamics of genetic dysregulation arising from inherited
variants, somatic mutations, epigenetic drift, and regulatory network
perturbations. It provides the foundational upstream signals that influence
pathogen kinetics (Module B), trauma physiology (Module C), and senescence
(Module D).

The module integrates:
\begin{itemize}
    \item regulatory network topology,
    \item variant effect propagation,
    \item transcription factor (TF) dynamics,
    \item epigenetic modulation,
    \item stochastic mutation processes.
\end{itemize}

\section{Regulatory Network Structure}

Let the regulatory network be represented as a directed graph:
\[
\mathcal{R} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E}),
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathcal{V}$ is the set of genes, TFs, and regulatory elements,
    \item $\mathcal{E}$ is the set of directed edges representing activation or repression.
\end{itemize}

Each edge $e_{mn} \in \mathcal{E}$ has:
\[
e_{mn} = (m \rightarrow n,\ k_{mn},\ \sigma_{mn}),
\]
where $k_{mn}$ is the interaction strength and $\sigma_{mn}$ is the regulatory mode
(activation or repression).

