\section{Variant Effect Mapping}

Let $\Delta_j$ denote the effect size of variant $j$.

Variant mapping operator:
\[
\Theta_A = \mathcal{T}_A(\mathbf{X}_{\text{NGS}}),
\]
where $\mathbf{X}_{\text{NGS}}$ is the genomic sequencing vector.

Interaction strength modification:
\[
k_{mn}
=
k_{mn}^{(0)}
+
\sum_{j}
\alpha_{mn,j}\Delta_j.
\]

Epigenetic drift:
\[
\epsilon_{mn}(t)
=
\epsilon_{mn}^{(0)}
+
\eta_{mn} t,
\]
with $\eta_{mn}$ representing drift rate.

\section{Gene Expression Dynamics}

Let $\mathbf{g}(t)$ be the gene expression vector.

The core dynamics are:
\[
\frac{d\mathbf{g}}{dt}
=
f_A(\mathbf{g}; \Theta_A)
=
\mathbf{W}\,\phi(\mathbf{g})
-
\mathbf{D}\mathbf{g}
+
\mathbf{b},
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathbf{W}$ is the regulatory weight matrix,
    \item $\phi(\cdot)$ is a nonlinear activation function,
    \item $\mathbf{D}$ is the degradation matrix,
    \item $\mathbf{b}$ is the basal transcription vector.
\end{itemize}

Activation function:
\[
\phi(g_i) = \frac{1}{1 + e^{-\beta(g_i - \theta_i)}}.
\]

\section{Transcription Factor Dynamics}

TF concentrations evolve according to:
\[
\frac{d\mathbf{t}}{dt}
=
\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}
-
\mathbf{B}\mathbf{t},
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathbf{A}$ maps gene expression to TF production,
    \item $\mathbf{B}$ is the TF degradation matrix.
\end{itemize}

Coupling:
\[
\mathbf{W}(t)
=
\mathbf{W}^{(0)}
+
\gamma \mathbf{t}(t).
\]

\section{Stochastic Mutation Process}

Somatic mutation rate:
\[
\mu(t)
=
\mu_0
+
\sigma_{\mu} dW_t,
\]
where $dW_t$ is a Wiener process.

Mutation impact on expression:
\[
\Delta \mathbf{g}(t)
=
\mathbf{M}\,\mu(t),
\]
with $\mathbf{M}$ representing mutation sensitivity.

\section{Module Output}

Module A outputs:
\[
\mathbf{S}_A(t)
=
\big(
\mathbf{g}(t),\,
\mathbf{t}(t),\,
\Theta_A
\big).
\]

This output feeds:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Module B (pathogen kinetics),
    \item Module C (trauma physiology),
    \item Module D (senescence).
\end{itemize}

\section{Coupling to Module B}

Immune modulation:
\[
\beta_1(t)
=
\beta_1^{(0)}
+
\lambda_1 \|\mathbf{g}(t)\|.
\]

Pathogen susceptibility:
\[
\chi(t)
=
\chi_0
+
\lambda_2 \phi(\mathbf{t}(t)).
\]

\section{Coupling to Module C}

Inflammatory response:
\[
\iota(t)
=
\iota_0
+
\lambda_3 \sum_i g_i(t).
\]

Shock sensitivity:
\[
\sigma_{\text{shock}}(t)
=
\sigma_0
+
\lambda_4 \|\mathbf{t}(t)\|.
\]

\section{Coupling to Module D}

Senescence acceleration:
\[
\gamma_{\text{sen}}(t)
=
\gamma_0
+
\lambda_5 \sum_i g_i(t).
\]

\section{Numerical Integration}

Module A uses adaptive RK45:
\[
\mathbf{z}(t_{k+1})
=
\mathbf{z}(t_k)
+
\Delta t_k\, \Phi_A(\mathbf{z}(t_k), t_k),
\]
with projection:
\[
\mathbf{z}_{\text{proj}} = \max(\mathbf{z}, 0).
\]

\section{Stress Testing}

High mutation stress:
\[
\mu_{\text{stress}}
=
10\mu_0.
\]

Regulatory collapse test:
\[
\mathbf{W}_{\text{stress}}
=
0.5\mathbf{W}^{(0)}.
\]

\section{Summary}

Module A provides the upstream genetic and regulatory signals that shape all
downstream biological modules. Its integration of variant effects, regulatory
network dynamics, TF modulation, and stochastic mutation processes makes it the
foundation of the MPS--PCE multiscale simulation engine.